        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 4,
        "titulo": "Genitive plural with numerals",
        "explicacao": "The big application: NUMBERS. Now we reach the most practical part of the genitive plural, and one of the most famous rules in Russian. The number 1 goes with the nominative singular. The numbers 2, 3 and 4 go with the genitive singular. The numbers 5 and up go with the genitive plural. English only has \"one book\" and \"two or more books\"; Russian has three groups. Let us see it with the word \"book\" (кни́га). Notice: the genitive singular is used with 2, 3, 4, and the genitive plural with 5+. Then with \"table\" (стол) and with \"window\" (окно́). Notice: the pattern is always the same. 1 → nominative singular. 2-4 → genitive singular. 5+ → genitive plural. The \"last number\" rule: when the number is compound, the last number decides. That is why Russian is logical: the last number of the sequence decides. Examples in sentences follow. The forms of \"1\" and \"2\" for each gender. The number 1 and the number 2: notice that the number 2 has два (masculine/neuter) and две (feminine). That is an important distinction. The numbers 3 and 4 do not change with gender: три, четы́ре.",
